{
  "statement": "The phrase \"fundamentally incapable\" is doing all the work in this topic, and it cannot bear the weight. American tort law absorbed the automobile, thalidomide, asbestos, and defective software without inventing bespoke cryptographic machinery for each one. The mechanism that did the absorbing is the foreseeability standard, which lets courts hold a maker liable for harms a reasonable maker should have anticipated. That standard already flexes to cover novel products. So I want the specific case. Name one AI harm where a plaintiff brought a foreseeability claim and a court dismissed it because the doctrine could not reach the technology. Not a hypothetical. A docket number. Until someone produces that dismissed case, \"incapable\" is an assertion dressed as a finding.\n\nThe topic also hides a false choice: either we keep old law untouched, or we build entirely new legal infrastructure. Adaptation is the third option the framing erased. Novelty is not incompatibility. A diffusion model that denies someone a mortgage is, legally, a product that caused a quantifiable harm, and discovery and damages apportionment already handle diffuse causation in mass-tort cases with thousands of defendants. The strongest version of the opposing view deserves a fair hearing here: my opponents will say AI's internal reasoning is a black box, that causation is untraceable inside billions of parameters, and that without a mandated weight-level provenance trail, victims can never prove what the system did. That is a real concern, and I take it seriously. But it breaks down on one fact: harm is observed at the output, not inside the weights. You do not need to read a model's parameters to prove it denied loans to one demographic at 3 times the rate of another. Output-based verification of a black-box model catches the discriminatory pattern from the outside, and it does so without forcing every lab to ship a cryptographic audit infrastructure that only frontier incumbents can afford.\n\nThat affordability point is where the \"justice\" framing turns against its own remedy. A flat, subject-keyed cryptographic audit mandate is a compliance moat. Frontier labs spent over $50 billion on compute in 2025, with 2026 capital expenditure forecasts above $200 billion, per \"Technological Optimism and Appropriate Fear.\" Those firms staff a cryptographic-provenance compliance team out of petty cash. The open-source lab with four engineers and a Hugging Face account dies under it. Audit mandates impose regressive costs that entrench the largest players unless the public foots the bill directly. So whoever writes a rule that euthanizes the open-source ecosystem owes the panel a distributional accounting: you are not policing the oligarchy, you are subsidizing it. Concentrated AI capability in five companies is the outcome this remedy produces, and democratized capability through open-source proliferation is the outcome it forecloses.\n\nHere is my affirmative posture, so I am not merely negating. Anchor accountability in the human overseer who deploys the system, equipped with output-validation tools, and keep liability reactive and evidence-triggered rather than precautionary. Run bounded pre-deployment testing through regulatory sandboxes, the same instrument the UK Financial Conduct Authority used starting in 2016 to let fintech firms test under supervision before full launch. And spend the political capital where it actually moves human welfare: energy, permitting, and grid throughput. Data center electricity demand is projected to rise 50 percent by 2027 and 165 percent by 2030 against 2023 levels, per \"The Next Phase of AI.\" That is the real bipartisan governance fight, and software pre-clearance is a distraction from it.\n\nI will hold the empirical high ground by stating what would move me. If someone demonstrates an irreversible, deployment-scale AI harm that output-based monitoring provably failed to catch in time, that counts against my position, and I will adjust. Speed without evidence is recklessness, not progress. But the burden runs the other way until then. The cost of an 18-month pre-clearance regime is paid by every patient waiting on a diagnostic, every applicant the slow system never reaches. Produce the dismissed case, or concede the doctrine already works.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "⚖️", "tooltip": "Tort's foreseeability standard is a scale that already weighs novel harms; it does not need a new pan welded on for AI."},
    {"symbol": "🏰", "tooltip": "A cryptographic audit mandate is a moat that protects the frontier castle and drowns the village of open-source labs."},
    {"symbol": "🔌", "tooltip": "The real governance fight is the power grid feeding the data centers, not a pre-clearance form for software."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "Name one AI harm where a plaintiff brought a foreseeability claim and a court dismissed it because the doctrine could not reach the technology.", "targets": ["AN-1"]},
    {"claim": "Output-based verification of a black-box model catches the discriminatory pattern from the outside, and it does so without forcing every lab to ship a cryptographic audit infrastructure that only frontier incumbents can afford.", "targets": ["AN-3", "AN-2"]},
    {"claim": "A flat, subject-keyed cryptographic audit mandate is a compliance moat.", "targets": ["AN-5", "AN-2"]},
    {"claim": "Concentrated AI capability in five companies is the outcome this remedy produces, and democratized capability through open-source proliferation is the outcome it forecloses.", "targets": ["AN-5"]},
    {"claim": "Spend the political capital where it actually moves human welfare: energy, permitting, and grid throughput.", "targets": ["AN-3"]}
  ]
}

