t FinTech regulatory sandbox in 2016, and we should copy that model for AI — relaxed rules in reversible zones with live monitoring, not permanent gates. Keep narrow, capability-tied carve-outs for chemical, biological, and offensive-cyber uses — the exact threats NIST's CAISI already evaluates. That is calibration, not abolition, and it pre-empts the \"one catastrophe triggers harsher law\" worry before it is raised.\n\nHere is where I move off a pure deregulatory line, because the distribution problem is real. Audits CAN be tiered — the EU AI Act carved out SME provisions, and NIST publishes risk tiers. Fine. So the answer is not \"no audits.\" It is no regressive private flat fee that entrenches oligarchs. Make any evaluation mandate publicly funded and risk-tiered, so a garage lab and a $200-billion incumbent do not face the same bill. There is even a technical path that keeps oversight cheap: the \"Monitoring Monitorability\" research shows a smaller model run at higher reasoning effort can match a larger model's capability while staying far easier to monitor. Accountability and speed are not enemies — bad mandate design makes them enemies.\n\nSo I put the burden back on the restrictors. If survival and a human off-switch justify freezing development, name the evidence. Is the threat an observed production behavior, or a whiteboard treacherous-turn scenario no deployed system has demonstrated? I will state my falsification bet plainly: an irreversible, deployment-scale harm that real-time output monitoring demonstrably failed to catch in time would count against me. That is a real bet with a real loss condition. Indefinite precaution has none. And it has a body count the survival framing never tallies — the patients, the workers, the people abundance reaches late because we chose hypothetical fear over measured speed. Show me the demonstrated harm, or own that cost.",
