{
  "statement": "The choice in front of Congress is not \"cryptographic audit trails or lawless AI\" — that's a false dilemma, and a costly one. The real fork is between two accountability designs: adapted liability anchored in what deployed systems actually do, or an 18-month flat-cost compliance apparatus that hands the frontier to three incumbents. I reject any mandated development pause outright. Accountability should track the observable behavior of a deployed system and the human who oversees it — not weight-level inspection of a model's internals, which neither regulators nor courts can read. Subject-keyed cryptographic audit trails sound rigorous. They are an engineering fantasy at frontier scale and a regressive tax on everyone who isn't OpenAI, Google, or Anthropic.\n\nHere is the cost-asymmetry the audit-trail proponents won't price. A cryptographic logging mandate is a fixed cost — you pay roughly the same to build subject-keyed provenance whether you ship to ten users or ten million. OpenAI absorbs that as a rounding error. A five-person open-weight lab eats it as a death sentence. The Skeptic will say tiering solves this, and the EU AI Act tiered its high-risk obligations, originally set for August 2, 2026. Fine — proportionate compliance can exist. But you cannot tier a cryptographic provenance requirement the way you tier paperwork: the crypto either binds every inference to a subject key or it doesn't, and the build cost lands before you have a single user to spread it across. Mandates that concentrate the frontier into three firms are not accountability. They are an antitrust event wearing a safety badge.\n\nSo name the mechanism. First, fintech-style regulatory sandboxes — the UK Financial Conduct Authority launched the first one in 2016, and the model now runs from Denmark's Data Protection Authority to the European Blockchain Sandbox. Bounded cohorts, real users, live supervision, reversible by design. Second, energy and permitting reform as the most tractable bipartisan lever in Washington: data-center electricity demand is projected to rise 165 percent by 2030 over 2023 levels, and that bottleneck is physical, not regulatory theater. Third, market-based insurance plus post-deployment liability with flexible foreseeability standards — accountability that follows the harm, scales with deployment, and keeps a human on the hook instead of automating responsibility into a legal fiction.\n\nThe Safetyist will say behavioral verification is gameable — that a deceptive model passes your output tests and defects later, the treacherous turn. That risk is real, and I won't wave it away. So pair output verification with continuous real-time monitoring rather than a one-time ex-ante gate. The research on monitorability shows you can run a smaller model at higher reasoning effort and recover most of the oversight a larger model would lose — what its authors call paying a \"monitorability tax\" — without freezing the capability frontier. Here is my falsification bet, stated plainly: an irreversible, deployment-scale harm that real-time monitoring demonstrably failed to catch in time would count against my position. I'll wager on monitoring I can audit before I'll wager on cryptography no court can read.\n\nNow the cost nobody on the mandate side will put on the table. Every quarter of pre-approval delay forecloses tangible welfare — diagnostic throughput, drug-discovery cycles, automated relief from drudgery — and that toll is paid by people who never get to vote in this room. The proponents of subject-keyed audit trails owe Congress two numbers they have not produced: the specific, demonstrated harm their mandate prevents, and the count of people abundance reaches more slowly because the mandate priced small labs out. Until they bring both, they are choosing who waits and who doesn't. Who gave them the right to make that call for everyone the technology would have reached first?",
