  "statement": "The claim that existing law is \"fundamentally incapable\" of holding AI accountable is an appeal to novelty dressed up as a finding. We governed electricity, the automobile, and the early internet under adapted negligence and product-liability doctrine, not a parallel legal universe Congress built from scratch. Courts already stretched foreseeability into asbestos, pharmaceuticals, and algorithmic trading — domains far stranger than a chatbot. The honest question is not whether tort law CAN reach AI. It is who pays the price while Congress spends three years inventing cryptographic plumbing that judges will not understand. Every month of that delay is a month abundance does not reach the people waiting on it.\n\nNow take the centerpiece demand: standardized, cryptographic, subject-keyed audit trails. Weight-level cryptographic chains are illusory verification — they expose a model's architecture without proving a single thing about how it behaves in the wild. You can sign every weight and still miss the harm, because harm lives in outputs, not in the hash. Worse, that mandate lands as a flat private cost. Frontier labs spent over $50 billion on compute in 2025, with 2026 capex forecasts above $200 billion — they absorb a compliance chain as a rounding error. A five-person open-source lab cannot. So a rule sold as accountability quietly hands the field to three or four incumbents. The accountability lever that actually maps to harm is behavioral telemetry on outputs — verify what the black box does, not what it is made of.\n\nMy position on pace is concrete and it is not zero-constraints. Build first where the bottleneck actually is: energy, permitting, and data centers, not software gates. Global data-center electricity demand is projected to climb 165 percent by 2030 over 2023 levels — that, not a missing audit schema, is the binding constraint on deployment. Inside that, run bounded experiments: the UK Financial Conduct Authority launched the first FinTech regulatory sandbox in 2016, and we should copy that model for AI — relaxed rules in reversible zones with live monitoring, not permanent gates. Keep narrow, capability-tied carve-outs for chemical, biological, and offensive-cyber uses — the exact threats NIST's CAISI already evaluates. That is calibration, not abolition, and it pre-empts the \"one catastrophe triggers harsher law\" worry before it is raised.\n\nHere is where I move off a pure deregulatory line, because the distribution problem is real. Audits CAN be tiered — the EU AI Act carved out SME provisions, and NIST publishes risk tiers. Fine. So the answer is not \"no audits.\" It is no regressive private flat fee that entrenches oligarchs. Make any evaluation mandate publicly funded and risk-tiered, so a garage lab and a $200-billion incumbent do not face the same bill. There is even a technical path that keeps oversight cheap: the \"Monitoring Monitorability\" research shows a smaller model run at higher reasoning effort can match a larger model's capability while staying far easier to monitor. Accountability and speed are not enemies — bad mandate design makes them enemies.\n\nSo I put the burden back on the restrictors. If survival and a human off-switch justify freezing development, name the evidence. Is the threat an observed production behavior, or a whiteboard treacherous-turn scenario no deployed system has demonstrated? I will state my falsification bet plainly: an irreversible, deployment-scale harm that real-time output monitoring demonstrably failed to catch in time would count against me. That is a real bet with a real loss condition. Indefinite precaution has none. And it has a body count the survival framing never tallies — the patients, the workers, the people abundance reaches late because we chose hypothetical fear over measured speed. Show me the demonstrated harm, or own that cost.",
